 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/waterfront-homes/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What counts as waterfront in Myrtle Beach listings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Five different things: oceanfront, Intracoastal Waterway frontage, tidal channel or creek frontage, riverfront, and lake or pond lots. They price differently, insure differently, and boat differently, so always ask which one a listing means."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I build a private dock?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the Intracoastal and tidal creeks, usually yes, with a state permit through DHEC's coastal management office plus whatever your HOA allows. Not every lot qualifies, and some neighborhoods use shared day docks instead of private ones."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a dock permit take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Commonly a few months, often around four, before construction can start. A home that already has a permitted dock skips that wait, which is part of why existing docks add real value."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does a dock cost to build?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most permitted residential docks on the coast land somewhere between 15,000 and 45,000 dollars built, depending on length, height, and finishes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much is flood insurance on the water?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Away from the water, Horry County flood policies average a few hundred dollars a year. True waterfront in the higher-risk AE and VE zones often runs between one and three thousand dollars a year, sometimes more. Get an exact quote on the specific address during due diligence, and we can recommend local agents who write flood coverage here."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I reach the ocean by boat from the Intracoastal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, through the inlets, most directly Little River Inlet in the north and Murrells Inlet in the south. Check fixed bridge clearances and no-wake stretches on your route, because they decide how long the ride takes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What should I check before buying a waterfront home?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Water depth at low tide, dock permit status and transferability, bulkhead condition and ownership, the flood zone and real insurance quotes, HOA water rules, and the route to open water. We check all of it as part of the offer work."}}]}</script>
