 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to come to South Carolina to close?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. You can sign the closing package in your own state before a notary, with two witnesses, or give someone a power of attorney. A South Carolina attorney still runs the closing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sign the deed on a video call with a notary?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not with a South Carolina notary. State law requires the signer and the notary to be in the same room. Ask the closing attorney whether they accept a video notarization done under another state's law."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who holds my earnest money?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually the closing attorney's trust account or the brokerage's escrow account. The contract says which. Confirm the wire instructions by phone before you send it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is my property tax higher than the seller's?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The seller may have paid the 4 percent legal-residence rate. A rental you do not live in is assessed at 6 percent. The bill is higher than the seller's for the same property."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need flood insurance if I pay cash?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A lender requires it in a high-risk zone. A cash buyer chooses. A new policy starts 30 days after purchase unless it is bought with a loan closing, so a cash buyer should buy it a month before closing."}}]}</script>
